# No hardcoded localhost in production code

Code that is deployed or bundled for production must **never** use hardcoded
`localhost` or `127.0.0.1`.

## Rules

- **Client (client/src)**: Do not use `localhost` or `127.0.0.1` in URLs,
  fetch calls, or API bases. Use relative paths (e.g. `/api/...`) or
  `process.env.REACT_APP_*` so production uses the same origin or a
  configured URL.
- **Server**: Do not assume the server runs on localhost when generating
  URLs or redirects for clients. Use config/env (e.g. `ALLOWED_ORIGINS`,
  `PORT`) so production can override.

## Allowed exceptions (do not add new ones)

- `client/package.json` — `"proxy"` is used only by the React dev server,
  not in the production build.
- Nginx/deploy configs that run on the server (e.g.
  `proxy_pass http://localhost:6001`) — same-host only, not app code.
- Local test/script files in repo root (e.g. `test-*.js`, `test-*.sh`) —
  not part of the deployed app.

## Correct patterns

- **API base URL**: `getApiBase()` / `REACT_APP_API_URL` (empty = same origin).
- **CORS / allowed origins**: `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` env in production; no
  localhost default in prod.
- **Debug/telemetry URLs**: Only when `NODE_ENV === 'development'` and an
  env var (e.g. `REACT_APP_DEBUG_INGEST_URL`) is set; never hardcode the host.

When adding new features or APIs, use environment variables or relative
paths so the same code works in dev and production without hardcoded hosts.
